The bottom tier was absorbing **27.8% of outbound call effort** and producing almost nothing. Top-tier leads applied at **eight times the rate**. Nobody had checked, because call volume was the metric everyone watched, and volume looked healthy.

The prompt was a capacity problem, not a curiosity. Leads were up **8.1%** while call output was down **16%**. Before anyone argued for more headcount, I asked where the existing effort was going. I pulled call attempts and lead outcomes from the CRM across seven months, measured 30-day application rates by tier on mature cohorts only, and split the timeline into three acts so a dialler backlog in the middle of the period could not masquerade as strategy.

The decision followed the evidence: stop calling the bottom tier. Effort on that tier fell to **0.7%**, conversion held, and the reclaimed capacity went to the leads that actually convert. The analysis also earned the mandate to replace fixed tiers with a probability model, which became its own workstream.
